# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout there lands these
# with CRLF while the generator that writes them — serde_json — emits LF
# unconditionally. `published_schema_is_current`, `canvas_fixtures_are_current`
# and `catalog_fixture_is_current` compare the checked-out bytes against the
# generated bytes exactly, and are right to: a fixture is a promise about
# bytes. The checkout is the thing that has to change, not the assertion.
#
# Scoped to *.json rather than a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`. Every file those
# tests read or regenerate is JSON — the published schema, the canvas
# fixtures, the node catalogue, and the starter definitions they migrate from
# — and every tracked .json blob is already LF in the index, so this attribute
# renormalises nothing, produces no diff on any platform, and cannot collide
# with work in flight. A blanket rule would rewrite the whole tree in one
# commit to fix three files.
#
# Not `git config --global core.autocrlf=false` on the CI runner, which would
# fix the same three tests: Demeteo writes that setting into the clones it owns
# (docs/WINDOWS_PARITY.md, phase 3) precisely to survive a Windows host whose
# global default is true. Flipping the runner's global default removes the
# condition that behaviour exists for, so its regression would go unseen.
*.json text eol=lf
